---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const extensions = (await getCollection('extensions')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
---

<Base
  title="Extensions — ubuos"
  description="An extension is a directory with one JSON file. Write it in anything; it runs as a process, not as a plugin."
>
  <section>
    <p class="pill">Extensions</p>
    <h1>One extension. Any number of packs.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      An <strong>extension</strong> is a capability: one <em>runs</em> things, one
      <em>remembers</em> them. There is no SDK and no interface to implement — it is a
      directory with one JSON file, run as a child process, so it can be written in
      anything.
    </p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:12px">
      A <strong>pack</strong> is what a runner runs against: the applications, the
      configurations, the versions. The engine does not know what it is testing, which is
      why pointing it at your project is one file rather than a fork.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="tight">
    <h2>Published</h2>
    {extensions.length > 0 ? (
      <div class="grid">
        {extensions.map((e) => (
          <article class="card">
            <h3>{e.data.name} <span class="pill">{e.data.kind}</span></h3>
            <p>{e.data.summary}</p>
            <pre class="mini"><code>{e.data.attach}</code></pre>
            {e.data.repo && <p class="more"><a href={e.data.repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source →</a></p>}
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    ) : (
      <div class="grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>None, and that is the point</h3>
          <p>
            The memory extension that used to sit here was folded into the tool.
            <code>oss memory</code> files, indexes and searches your notes with nothing
            attached, so there is no longer anything to install to get it.
          </p>
          <pre class="mini"><code>oss memory file notes.md
oss memory search "connection pool"</code></pre>
          <p class="more">
            An extension is worth writing when <code>oss</code> genuinely cannot know
            something — what is installed on your machine, and how your project runs.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    )}
  </section>

  <section class="tight">
    <h2>Packs — nothing to attach</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      A pack is data. It has no program to call, so there is nothing to register: you name
      the directory when you run it, or you stand in it.
    </p>

    <div class="grid">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Yours <span class="pill">pack</span></h3>
        <p>
          A pack is a description of your applications, the versions you care about and
          the configurations to run them under — so “does this reproduce” has an answer
          rather than an opinion. Its whole contract is one <code>pack.sh</code>: four
          declarations and a function that maps an application name to its directory.
        </p>
        <pre class="mini"><code>oss run --pack ~/my-project \
  matrix --scenario startup</code></pre>
        <p class="more"><a href="https://ubuos.com/docs/connect/">How to write one →</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:16px">
      Packs are not published here and do not need to be. The matrix engine ships inside
      <code>oss</code>; a pack is data it reads off your disk, so there is no manifest, no
      program and nothing to register. <code>oss ext add</code> on a pack fails, correctly.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="tight">
    <h2>Writing one</h2>
    <p class="lede">Put this at the root of your repository, and paste the path.</p>

    <pre><code>{`{
  "name": "kafka",
  "kind": "runner",
  "description": "My own Kafka setup — brokers on localhost:9092",
  "exec": "./kbench",
  "verbs": { "list": "list", "run": "run" }
}`}</code></pre>

    <div class="notes">
      <div>
        <h3>Two kinds</h3>
        <p>
          A <strong>runner</strong> executes something real. A <strong>memory</strong>
          remembers. They are the two questions the core cannot answer alone.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Verbs are a mapping</h3>
        <p>
          The left side is what the core says; the right side is what your tool calls it.
          You never have to rename your own commands to join in.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Writing outward is refused</h3>
        <p>
          If a verb of yours posts somewhere public, declare it. It is then refused unless
          the person names the repository on the command line and confirms it — every time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <pre class="mini"><code>oss ext add /path/to/your/repo
oss ext list</code></pre>
  </section>

  <style>
    .tight { padding-top: 0; }
    .mini { margin-top: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; }
    .more { margin-top: 14px; }
    .more a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }
    .notes { display: grid; gap: 24px; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); margin: 30px 0; }
    .notes p { color: var(--muted); margin: 0; }
  </style>
</Base>
